these volunteers want to regain the lost  beauty of their city  they're removing rubbish littering the  banks of the tigris river in baghdad  to make it cleaner and healthier we will  keep going until  everybody gets the message until people  realize that  it's our civil responsibility to take  care of our public places and to protect  them to not throw trash that throwing  trash on the street in the river is an  offense even if currently the law  doesn't  penalize you for it that we should use  have our own morals and our own  discipline to to protect these places  the river banks are a breathing space  for many here in iraq's capital  especially those in its suburbs  but with rubbish pining up in several  cities along the river  and with it becoming increasingly  polluted  groups like this are out to get levels  of waste down  this amount of rubbish can also be  reduced  by making  people more aware of the river's  importance to ecology and for recreation  that could help reduce littering  but the river would still remain  contaminated by other activities  like wastewater  with neglected infrastructure tons of  garbage and waste fall into the river  including metals and debris  and even dead animals  some waste breaks down and forms harmful  micro plastics  safe transfers passengers between the  river banks on his boat  it's a family business he inherited from  his father but it's now in decline  it is my second home but i feel bad  every day when i watch all these  polluters around people have become less  attracted to boat tours and tourism is  now zero  with population growth and urban  expansion in baghdad  more sewage and waste is being  discharged into the river  healthy facilities oil installations  factories and laboratories  all dump toxic waste directly into the  tigris  officials say the amount of  contamination is alarming the is  there are 25 sewage plants in baghdad  discharging directly into the river  without treatment a permanent solution  to this requires transformation of the  whole sewage system in baghdad that  means a lot of money and effort it could  take years  the tigris is a main source of drinking  water in baghdad  and these volunteers want to restore its  cleanliness and vitality  and encourage others across iraq to do  the same  al jazeera
 

